dialect protocol
protocol deflate.snapshot
version 1
schema stdio.deflate
start document
framing record

# P2-FG2: real RFC1950 zlib container layout, per this artifact's own real decoder
# (`decode_deflate_snapshot`, ⚙️engine/component.rs:596-645) -- CMF/FLG are two genuinely,
# individually byte-walked header fields; RFC1950 §2.2's own bit layout inside each:
#   CMF: bits 0-3 = compression method (CM, this codec only accepts 8 = deflate), bits 4-7 =
#        compression info (CINFO, window size = 2^(CINFO+8)).
#   FLG: bits 0-4 = FCHECK, bit 5 = FDICT (preset-dictionary flag), bits 6-7 = FLEVEL
#        (compression-level hint, informational only).
# RFC1950 mandates `(CMF*256+FLG) % 31 == 0` -- a real constraint this artifact's own codec
# enforces (`decode_deflate_snapshot`'s own check, engine/component.rs:607-609) but NOT encodable
# here: `Cond` (`if <field> eq/ne/lt/le/gt/ge <literal>`) can only compare ONE already-decoded
# field's WHOLE value against a static literal -- no bitwise-AND/modulo operator exists, and this
# constraint spans TWO fields (cmf AND flg together), which `Cond` cannot reference jointly
# either. Document the constraint, don't fabricate a dialect assertion that can't express it.
#
# NOTE: this ONE `header` block ends up holding all four fields below (`cmf`/`flg`/`body`/
# `adler32`), not just the 2 truly-fixed-width leading bytes -- no intervening `segment`/`record`
# keyword closes it before `body`/`adler32` (`parse_protocol`'s own `close_header` only fires at
# EOF or the next explicit block-opening keyword), and `header fixed N`'s own `N` is parsed but
# never validated/enforced by `walk_protocol` (confirmed by direct reading of `parse_protocol`'s
# `"header"` arm: `let _size = parse_u64_literal(...)`, underscore-prefixed, unused). `fixed 2`
# documents CMF+FLG's own genuinely-fixed-width prefix; keeping all four fields in this ONE block
# (rather than splitting `body`/`adler32` into a separate `segment`) is deliberate -- see the
# `body`/`adler32` field-reservation comment below for why a split would need `Block::Chain`,
# which this dialect's own cross-block reservation doesn't correctly support.
header fixed 2
field cmf u8
field flg u8

# RFC1950's optional 4-byte DICTID is present iff FLG's FDICT bit (bit 5) is set -- real
# conditional presence, but genuinely NOT expressible via `Cond` either: FDICT's bit-5
# contribution to the FLG byte's numeric value isn't isolable from FLEVEL's bits 6-7 by any
# single eq/ne/lt/le/gt/ge threshold test (no bitwise-AND primitive exists in this dialect,
# confirmed live -- `Cond`'s only operators compare a field's WHOLE value; see
# `📖️grammar-recipe.md` §2.2's own `Cond` section). SAME mechanism gap already hit and
# documented by this program's own prior wave for an analogous multi-bit `flags` byte
# (`../../../../../../📰️xml/🏅️standards/🔖️1.0/🪆️subsets/✳️any/🧬️schema/🔺️diff/💾️binary/
# 📡️.protocol.semio`'s own `format`/`flags` header comment) -- filed here as
# `protocol-cond-no-bitmask` (this wave's report `mechanism_gaps`). Modeled honestly: `body`
# below folds the (rare, optional) DICTID prefix into the SAME opaque boundary the compressed
# DEFLATE bitstream already, independently, needs (Huffman/LZ77 codes are variable-bit-length
# and cross byte boundaries -- out of reach for any byte-oriented protocol dialect regardless of
# this gap). The real Rust codec (`encode_deflate_snapshot`/`decode_deflate_snapshot`) still
# fully, genuinely types `dict_id` on its own -- only THIS protocol description folds it into
# the opaque tail. `adler32` past it is real again: a genuine, individually protocol-walked
# trailing field (RFC1950 §2.3, BE per this codec's own `to_be_bytes()`/`from_be_bytes()`,
# engine/component.rs:522,543,578,581,618,629) -- kept in the SAME `header` block as `cmf`/`flg`
# (not a separate `segment`/`chain` block) so `walk_fields`'s own per-block field reservation
# math (every field's greedy-`bytes` reserves `sum(prim_fixed_width)` of every FOLLOWING field in
# the SAME field list) leaves exactly the trailing 4 bytes for it -- `Block::Chain`'s own
# cross-block reservation is NOT usable for this (confirmed by direct reading: `walk_protocol`'s
# `Block::Chain` arm hardcodes `reserved_tail=0` for its own walk regardless of what
# `trailing_reserved` computes for later blocks, so two separate `chain <name> <prim>`
# declarations stacked back-to-back would NOT correctly reserve room for the second one; a single
# field LIST inside one `header`/`segment` block is the only construct this dialect gets right).
field body bytes
field adler32 u32be
